Final JavaScript project: a working **to-do list** — the classic first interactive app. It combines DOM, events, arrays, and functions.

### The plan
1. Read the value from an input field
2. On form submit, add the task to a list
3. Render the list by creating `<li>` elements
4. Clear the input so the user can type the next task

### Creating elements dynamically
```js
const li = document.createElement("li");
li.textContent = task;
list.appendChild(li);
```

### Reading input
```js
const input = document.querySelector("#task-input");
const task = input.value;
```

### Resetting the input after adding
```js
input.value = "";   // clear the field
input.focus();      // put the cursor back
```

### The full flow
```js
const form = document.querySelector("#todo-form");
const list = document.querySelector("#task-list");

form.addEventListener("submit", (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();              // no page reload
  const input = document.querySelector("#task-input");
  const task = input.value.trim();
  if (!task) return;                    // ignore empty

  const li = document.createElement("li");
  li.textContent = task;
  list.appendChild(li);

  input.value = "";
  input.focus();
});
```

Key ideas: `event.preventDefault()` stops the reload, `trim()` removes stray spaces, and the empty check prevents blank tasks. This same flow powers hundreds of real apps.
